      body:
        "Hitler did not win power. He was **appointed** — the result of deals among a small group of powerful men around President **Paul von Hindenburg**, the 85-year-old First World War field marshal.\n\nFrom 1930 no coalition could command a Reichstag majority, so chancellors governed using the President's **Article 48** emergency decrees. That made the President and his advisers the real power-brokers.\n\n| Chancellor | In office | What happened |\n|---|---|---|\n| Heinrich Brüning | Mar 1930 – May 1932 | Cut spending and benefits during the Depression; ruled by decree; nicknamed the 'Hunger Chancellor' |\n| Franz von Papen | Jun – Nov 1932 | Almost no Reichstag support; called the July 1932 election that made the Nazis the largest party |\n| Kurt von Schleicher | Dec 1932 – Jan 1933 | An army general and schemer; failed to split the Nazis or build a majority |\n| Adolf Hitler | from 30 Jan 1933 | Leader of the largest party |\n\nIn August 1932 Hitler demanded to be Chancellor. Hindenburg, who looked down on the former corporal, refused. In November 1932 the Nazis lost 34 seats and the party was short of money; their rise seemed to be stalling.\n\nThen Papen, eager for revenge on Schleicher, who had helped bring him down, met Hitler secretly on **4 January 1933** at a banker's house in Cologne. They agreed a deal: Hitler as Chancellor, Papen as Vice-Chancellor. Papen and Hindenburg's son Oskar persuaded the President. Only **three Nazis** — Hitler, Wilhelm Frick and Hermann Göring — sat in the new cabinet; the rest were conservatives.\n\n> “In two months we'll have pushed Hitler so far into a corner that he'll squeak.” — remark attributed to Franz von Papen, January 1933\n\nThe conservatives thought they had 'hired' Hitler for his popular support. They were disastrously wrong.",
