      body:
        "Historians explain Hitler's rise by weighing several types of cause — and showing how they **linked**.\n\n| Type of cause | Examples |\n|---|---|\n| Long-term | Bitterness at defeat and the Treaty of Versailles; a young democracy with weaknesses (proportional representation, unstable coalitions, Article 48); army officers, judges and landowners who never accepted democracy |\n| Short-term crisis | The Great Depression from 1929: about 6 million unemployed by 1932; fear of Communism |\n| Nazi strengths | Hitler's charismatic speaking; Goebbels' propaganda; the SA; a nationwide party organisation; money from some wealthy backers |\n| The final trigger | Backstairs intrigue: Papen and Hindenburg appointed Hitler, believing they could control him |\n\nYour Term 2 enquiry asks: *under what circumstances did dictatorships come to power?* Hitler's case fits a pattern of three linked conditions:\n\n1. **Crisis** — the Depression made millions desperate enough to try extreme answers.\n2. **Weak government** — Weimar's coalitions collapsed, so presidents ruled by decree, and a few men around Hindenburg could decide who governed.\n3. **Charismatic leadership** — Hitler and his propaganda machine turned anger and fear into votes.\n\nNo single factor was enough. Without the Depression, the Nazis were a fringe party with 12 seats (1928). Without Hitler and Nazi organisation, the anger could have gone elsewhere — to the Communists, for example. And without the elites' decision in January 1933, taken when Nazi votes were *falling*, Hitler might never have become Chancellor at all.\n\nA strong answer chooses the **most important** factor and justifies the choice with a test. Which factor made the others matter? Which one, if removed, would most clearly have stopped Hitler?",
